+### 2300 — mac-keyvault T3 rev2: a hello that attests a key must bind the stream to that key, and a documented code set must be enumerated against the corpus
+- FINDING (review rev1 F1): `SignerServer` resolved the address again on every request with the requested version (0 = newest), so a `rotate` after the hello silently moved `pub`/`sign`/`verify` to v2 while the consumer had attested the v1 fingerprint — a bypass around the hello attestation, reproduced by the reviewer against the real binary. Fix: `Serve` pins the generation the hello resolved (`pinGeneration`, `s.bound`) and every handler addresses exactly that generation; the hello `version` is now the bound generation (never 0) and a new generation needs a new session. `signerclient.Start` refuses a hello with `version` 0 or a version other than the requested one (`ErrProtocol`), so an old server cannot be consumed unnoticed. Regressions: `TestSignerServePinsGenerationAcrossRotate` (pipe-driven, rotate between hello and first request, store `Sign` label asserted, unpinned-after-rotate and `--version 1` controls) and the built-binary e2e (`TestClientAgainstBuiltBinaryAndLoginKeychain` now rotates the throwaway key mid-session and deletes both generations by version). Mutants: pub-only re-resolution (M1) and per-request resolution restored (M2) are killed; client gate removed (M5) and narrowed to `<` (M6) are killed.
+- FINDING (review rev1 F2): the golden corpus carried 10 of the 13 documented stream codes; `metadata_unknown`, `unsupported_primitive` and `security` had unit coverage but no byte-exact wire evidence, and "one negative per error code" was prose. Fix: the closed set is now data (`signerclient.StreamErrorCodes`, 13 codes) and `TestSignerGoldenCoversStreamErrorCodes` compares it with the corpus in both directions and logs the ratio (13 of 13); four sessions were added — `unreadable` (garbage tag → `metadata_unknown` on sign and verify), `no-row` (`store: enclave`, no registry row → `unsupported_primitive`), `no-spki` (public half unreadable → `security` on pub and verify, hello fingerprint empty), `corrupt` (backend signature that does not verify under the key → `security` on sign). Each session ends with a successful request so the stream-continues property is byte-exact too. Mutants: a code dropped from the list (M3) and both fixtures of a code removed (M4) are killed. Rule: a documented enumeration is a list in code with a test that walks it; README repeats the list but is not the source.
+- ANOMALY: one run of `go test ./...` had the e2e `rotate` answer `not_found` ("no version exists for any kind") one second after `init` succeeded and the signer hello had resolved the same key; five isolated reruns and a full rerun passed. Suspected transient keychain listing under concurrent test-label churn from the sibling packages' live-keychain suites; not reproduced, not fixed, recorded as a stated bound (the flake is in listing, not in the pin).
+
+### 2140 — mac-keyvault T3: the signer contract is the CLI's gates on a stream, pinned byte for byte, and mac-keyvault defines it because kvctl does not exist yet
+- DECISION: kvctl (bsim `EPIC-260915-3slg4l`) is not code, so mac-keyvault defines the machine contract and ships the client kvctl will vendor: `mac-keyvault signer serve --address <s>/<p> [--kind] [--version N]` speaks **contract 1** as JSON lines — hello `{contract:1, ok, result{tool, tool_version, address, kind, version, label, fingerprint, ops}}`, then `{id, op: describe|pub|sign|verify, …}` → `{id, ok, result|error{code, message, hint, os_status?}}` in order. A one-shot CLI contract was rejected: kvctl signs many times per PKI run and each CLI call re-lists the keychain; a persistent server also lets the consumer check `fingerprint` once. The server (`internal/keyvault/signer.go`) is a thin loop over the same `Manager.Describe/Sign/PublicKeyFor` the CLI uses, so `usages`, `validity`, the trust gate and the prefix guard apply unchanged; the address is parsed by `ParseAddress`, so a raw label is refused before the vault is opened (mutant M8, a raw label inside the prefix admitted, is killed by `TestRunSignerServeRefusesBeforeStore`).
+- DECISION: Errors never end the stream and a startup failure is one line. A line that is not one JSON object is `bad_request` under `id: null`; the id is read first from an open map so a request with a member outside the contract is still answered under its own id (`bad_request`); `missing_id` covers absent, null and non-scalar ids; `unknown_op` matches exactly (`SIGN` ≠ `sign`, M2 killed). Every pre-server failure of `signer serve` (usage, `foreign_label`, no vault) is written as the same one-line hello with `ok: false` — the first version wrote the multi-line CLI envelope and the client read `{` as the hello (caught by the e2e test, fixed by `WriteStartupFailure`; M11 restoring text mode for usage is killed). EOF exits 0; blank lines are skipped.
+- FINDING: Golden fixtures (`internal/keyvault/testdata/signer-v1/*.json`, 35 fixture files plus the key material, one request/response each, 17 negative responses across every server-side code plus `usage_refused`/`expired`/`not_found` sessions) need a deterministic backend: ECDSA is randomised, so the fixtures carry one P-256 SPKI, one digest and one low-S DER signature (`fixture-key.json`) that a canned store returns for exactly that digest and the test re-verifies before trusting the fixtures. `json.Marshal` HTML-escapes `<` in hints; the wire uses `SetEscapeHTML(false)` so a line reads as written. `-update` regenerates responses; a change of bytes is a contract change.
